```js
updated(changedProperties) {
  if (changedProperties.has('error') || changedProperties.has('validity')) {
    const invalid = this.validity && this.validity !== 'valid';

    this.items.forEach((el) => {
      if (this.error) el.setAttribute('error', '');
      else el.removeAttribute('error');
    });

    const fieldset = this.shadowRoot.querySelector('fieldset');
    if (invalid) {
      fieldset.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
      fieldset.setAttribute('aria-describedby', 'error-message');
    } else {
      fieldset.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
      fieldset.removeAttribute('aria-describedby');
    }
  }
}

render() {
  return html`
    <fieldset role="radiogroup">
      <legend><slot name="legend"></slot></legend>
      <slot></slot>
      ${this.error
        ? html`<p id="error-message" role="alert" aria-live="assertive">${this.error}</p>`
        : nothing}
    </fieldset>
  `;
}
```

Key points:
- `aria-invalid="true"` on the `fieldset` (the element with `role="radiogroup"`) makes the invalid state discoverable on focus/navigation, not just at the moment the error first appears (a `role="alert"` region only fires once, on insertion — a screen reader user tabbing back into an already-invalid group later hears nothing without this).
- `aria-describedby="error-message"` ties the visible error text to the group so it's read as part of the group's accessible description, and gives the `<p>` a stable `id` to point to.
- Reacting to `validity` too (not just `error`) means `valueMissing` (required-but-unanswered) also gets flagged, since that state may not set `error` at all.
